]\nL117: \nL118: Time Zone\nL119: \nL120: The program is currently displayed in (GMT+11:00) Hobart.\nL121: \nL122: [Input]Use conference time zone: (GMT+11:00) Hobart[Input]Select other time zone\nL123: \nL124: [Select]\nL125: \nL126: The GMT offsets shown reflect the offsets at the moment of the conference.\nL127: \nL128: Time Band\nL129: By setting a time band, the program will dim events that are outside this time window. This is useful for (virtual) conferences with a continuous program (with repeated sessions).\nL130: The time band will also limit the events that are included in the personal iCalendar subscription service.\nL131: \nL132: [Input]Display full program[Input]Specify a time band\nL133: \nL134: [Input] - [Input]\nL135: \nL136: Save\nL137: \nL138: 【2†Close】\nL139: \nL140: When\nL141: \nL142: Tue 3 Feb 2026 14:10 - 14:30 at 【86†Pyrmont】 - 【87†Parallel Algorithms】 Chair(s): 【88†Kenjiro Taura】\nL143: \nL144: Abstract\nL145: Zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) are cryptographic protocols that allow verification of statements without disclosing the underlying information. Among them, PLONK-based ZKPs are particularly notable for offering succinct, non-interactive proofs of knowledge with a universal trusted setup, leading to widespread adoption in blockchain and cryptocurrency applications. Nonetheless, their broader deployment is hindered by long proof-generation times and substantial memory demands.\nL146: While GPUs can accelerate these computations, their limited memory capacity introduces significant challenges for efficient end-to-end proof generation.\nL147: This paper presents \\textit{Pipelonk}, a GPU-accelerated framework for end-to-end PLONK proof generation with two key contributions. First, \\textit{Pipelonk} introduces a segmentable operator library that offloads all operations, including those not trivially parallelized, to GPUs through new designs. Each operator supports segmented execution, allowing inputs to be divided into smaller segments processed independently, thus enabling large-scale computations on memory-constrained devices.\nL148: Second, \\textit{Pipelonk} provides a pipeline executor that overlaps computation and data transfer. It globally schedules compute- and memory-intensive tasks while preserving data and security dependencies, balances transfer-latency hiding against peak memory, and adaptively selects per-operator segment sizes by modeling memory capacity and computational characteristics to maximize compute-transfer overlap.\nL149: Evaluation shows that \\textit{Pipelonk} runs efficiently on devices with \\SI{8}{GB} to \\SI{80}{GB} memory, achieving an average speedup of 10.7$\\times$ and up to 19.4$\\times$ over the state-of-the-art baseline.\nL150: DOI\nL151: \nL152: 【89†https://doi.org/10.1145/3774934.3786448†doi.org】\nL153: \nL154: 【79†Zhiyuan Zhang Shandong University】\nL155: \nL156: 【80†Yanxin Cai Shandong University】\nL157: \nL158: 【81†Wenhao Yin Shandong University】\nL159: \nL160: 【82†Xueyu Wu The University of Hong Kong】\nL161: \nL162: 【83†Yi Wang Shenzhen University】\nL163: \nL164: 【84†Lei Ju Shandong University China】\nL165: \nL166: 【85†Zhuoran Ji Shandong University】\nL167: ### Program Display Configuration\nL168: \nL169: [Input][Input]\nL170: \nL171: Time Zone\nL172: \nL173: The program is currently displayed in (GMT+11:00) Hobart.\nL174: \nL175: [Input]Use conference time zone: (GMT+11:00) Hobart[Input]Select other time zone\nL176: \nL177: [Select]\nL178: \nL179: The GMT offsets shown reflect the offsets at the moment of the conference.\nL180: \nL181: Time Band\nL182: By setting a time band, the program will dim events that are outside this time window. This is useful for (virtual) conferences with a continuous program (with repeated sessions).\nL183: The time band will also limit the events that are included in the personal iCalendar subscription service.\nL184: \nL185: [Input]Display full program[Input]Specify a time band\nL186: \nL187: [Input] - [Input]\nL188: \nL189: S