-proving/)\nciteturn3view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"pattern\":\"2026-09-01\"}); Total lines: 177\nL0:   * 【0†Home】\nL1:   * 【1†Areas】\nL2:   * 【2†Tags】\nL3:   * 【3†RSS】\nL4:   * 【4†CSD†csd.cmu.edu】\nL5: \nL6: 【5†Image: CSD Logo】\nL7: \nL8: 【6†CSD PhD Blog】\nL9: \nL10:   * 【0†Home】\nL11:   * 【1†Areas】\nL12:   * 【2†Tags】\nL13:   * 【3†RSS】\nL14:   * 【4†CSD†csd.cmu.edu】\nL15: ## Contents\nL16: \nL17:   * 【7†How to Verify Private (or Expensive) Computations】\nL18:   * 【8†Sum-Check Protocol Overview】\nL19:   * 【9†Brief Interlude on Multilinear Polynomials】\nL20:     * 【10†Example: Arithmetizing a Batched Zero-Check】\nL21:   * 【11†Existing Algorithms for Sum-Check】\nL22:     * 【12†Linear-time algorithm】\nL23:     * 【13†Streaming algorithm with logarithmic space】\nL24:   * 【14†New Idea: Round Batching and its Benefits】\nL25:     * 【15†Why does round batching help?】\nL26:   * 【16†Conclusion】\nL27:   * 【17†Citations:】\nL28: # 【18†Algorithms for the Sum-check Protocol】\nL29: \nL30: 2026-09-01 | 【19†Quang Dao†quangvdao.github.io】\nL31: ## How to Verify Private (or Expensive) Computations\nL32: \nL33: Suppose a hospital runs a statistical analysis over millions of patient records to determine whether a drug is effective. The computation takes days of cluster time, and the raw data cannot be shared due to privacy regulations. A regulatory agency needs to trust the result—but cannot re-run the computation or inspect the data. How can they be confident the analysis was performed correctly?\nL34: This is an instance of a broader challenge: we increasingly rely on computational results we cannot feasibly re-check. Sometimes the data is private, so other parties cannot re-run the computation at all. Other times the data is public but the computation is simply too expensive to repeat.\nL35: Cryptographic zero-knowledge proof systems address this dilemma. They let an untrusted prover convince a verifier that some private data satisfies a public statement. For instance, you can prove that you are over 18 or that your account has sufficient balance without revealing your birthdate or financial history. Proofs are short and fast to verify—milliseconds rather than hours or days—so the savings compound when many parties need to check the same result.\nL36: Over the past decade, cryptographic proof systems have matured from theoretical curiosities into practical tools, with applications ranging from age verification [1] to blockchain scalability [2]. However, generating a proof is still orders of magnitude slower than running the computation natively, making prover efficiency a central challenge.\nL37: Many state-of-the-art proof systems owe much of their performance to a classical protocol from 1992: the sum-check protocol [4]. Sum-check’s very success, however, has made it a bottleneck: it often consumes the majority of the total proving time. In this blog post, I will introduce the sum-check protocol, detail well-known algorithms for its prover, and present a new technique that speeds up the prover in practical settings [9].\nL38: ## Sum-Check Protocol Overview\nL39: \nL40: The sum-check protocol [4] is an interactive proof that allows an untrusted prover to convince a computationally limited verifier of the value of a very large sum. Informally, the verifier wants to check a sum of many evaluations of a multivariate polynomial over a large product domain, but would like to avoid explicitly computing all of those values.\nL41: We will require two properties from the protocol: completeness (an honest prover always convinces the verifier) and soundness (a cheating prover is caught with overwhelming probability). After describing the protocol, we will verify that sum-check satisfies both.\nL42: Formally, we fix a finite field \\( \\mathbb{F} \\) (a number system with addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division, but only finitely many elements) and a multivariate polynomial \\( p(X_1, \\dots, X_n) \\in \\mathbb{F}[X_1,\\dots, X_n] \\), of degree bounded by \\( d \\) in each variable. The sum-check claim is then $$ \\sum_{x_1 \\in H_1, \\dots, x_n \\in H_n} p(x_1,\\dots,x_n) = c, $$ for some evaluation domains \\( H_1, \\dots, H_n \\subseteq \\mathbb{F} \\) and a claimed value \\( c \\in \\mathbb{F} \\).\nL43: In most applications, and for the remainder of this blog post, we restrict the inputs to the Boolean hypercube , namely the domain \\( H_1 = \\dots = H_n = \\{0,1\\} \\). The claimed value \\( c \\) remains an arbitrary element of \\( \\mathbb{F} \\).\nL44: The verifier knows \\( p \\), or at least has query access to \\( p \\) (meaning it can ask for evaluations of \\( p \\) at chosen points), but wants to use this access as little as possible. Naively, the verifier could evaluate \\( p \\) on all \\( 2^n \\) points of \\( \\{0,1\\}^n \\) and sum the results, which takes work on the order of \\( O(2^n) \\).\nL45: The key idea of sum-check is that, by interacting with an untrusted prover who supplies additional “auxiliary” polynomials, the verifier can reduce the work of checking the original claim to checking a related claim about \\( p \\) at a single randomly chosen point.\nL46: In particular, the data that the prover sends at each round are the “one-dimensional” slices of this multivariate polynomial. In the first round, the prover sends the univariate polynomial $$ s_1(X) = \\sum_{(x_2,\\dots,x_n) \\in \\{0,1\\}^{n-1}} p(X, x_2,\\dots,x_n). $$ If the original claim is correct, then \\( s_1(X) \\) has degree at most \\( d \\), and moreover \\( s_1(0) + s_1(1) = c \\). The verifier checks precisely these two conditions, and rejects if either fails.\nL47: If both checks pass, the verifier samples a random challenge \\( r_1 \\gets \\mathbb{F} \\) and sends it to the prover. This random point forces consistency: if the prover sent an incorrect low-degree \\( s_1 \\), then with high probability (at least \\( 1 - d / \\lvert \\mathbb{F} \\rvert \\)) it disagrees with the honest slice at \\( X=r_1 \\). Over a sufficiently large field (e.g., at least 128-bit), this error probability is negligible. We discuss why this is the case under soundness below.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReleases · ingonyama-zk/icicle · GitHub (https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/releases)\nciteturn3view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"pattern\":\"transpose fusion\"}); Total lines: 877\nL222:   * [FEAT] MatMul API by 【108†@lnm-at-work】 in 【109†#871】\nL223:   * [FEAT] Expose Sumcheck protocol challenge vector by 【102†@LeonHibnik】 in 【110†#946】\nL224:   * Timur/pqc go wrappers by 【99†@nonam3e】 in 【111†#944】\nL225:   * [FEAT] Add flattening utilities for polynomial slices into scalar slices by 【88†@yshekel】 in 【112†#952】\nL226:   * [CI] Separate testing by feature by 【93†@jeremyfelder】 in 【113†#913】\nL227:   * [FEAT] Add reinterpretation utilities and polynomial vector operations by 【88†@yshekel】 in 【114†#954】\nL228:   * [FIX] typo in ci.yml by 【88†@yshekel】 in 【115†#959】\nL229:   * [REFACTOR] move matrix transpose to matrix_ops, add PolyRing support and tests by 【88†@yshekel】 in 【116†#961】\nL230:   * [CI] Fix Go tests and Rust bn254 by 【93†@jeremyfelder】 in 【117†#960】\nL231:   * [CI] Move merkle test to hash crate by 【93†@jeremyfelder】 in 【118†#963】\nL232:   * Determine the scalar size according to configuration if provided by 【119†@mickeyasa】 in 【120†#955】\nL233:   * [FEATURE] Pseudorandom field element sampling from seed by 【121†@ChickenLover】 in 【122†#950】\nL234:   * Validate JL projection consistency across backends by 【100†@dorshmil】 in 【123†#956】\nL235:   * devdocs: June 15 Update by 【90†@Shalmoni】 in 【124†#947】\nL236:   * consistent norm check by 【96†@emirsoyturk】 in 【125†#967】\nL237:   * Devdocs quick fix by 【90†@Shalmoni】 in 【126†#969】\nL238:   * [FIX] fix int devision bug in MSM by 【1